    {
      "issue": "The `expiry` case never reaches idle expiry, so it is a duplicate of the `list` case and the test's closing claim (\"including close and expiry\") is not backed by any assertion. Traced by reading, not by running (no shell in this session): `listWorkers` calls `refresh(w)` first (lib/agent.js:613); `refresh` calls `checkGitIntegrity`, which finds the swapped `COMMIT_EDITMSG` and overwrites the hand-set `status: 'done'` with `'stuck'` (lib/agent.js:61) before returning. `expire` then sees `w.status !== 'done'` (lib/agent.js:606) and returns without calling `closeWorker`. The `catch (error.kind !== 'git_tamper')` branch in `expire` (lib/agent.js:608) is therefore never executed by this test, and the stale `lastActivityAt` has no effect. The test would pass unchanged if `expire`'s tamper handling were deleted or broken.",
      "file": "tests/agent-git-tamper.mjs",
      "line": "63",
      "quote": "if(api==='list'||api==='expiry') reported(listWorkers(ctx).find(r=>r.id===w.id),file);",
      "suggestion": "Make the expiry case reach `expire` → `closeWorker` with the tamper first seen there: keep the record `done` and idle, and plant the symlink so the first check that sees it is the one inside `closeWorker` (for example through an injected hook or by calling the expiry path after a clean `refresh`), then assert `record(w).surfaceClosed === true` and that the returned row still carries `kind: 'git_tamper'`. If that path is only reachable by a race, drop the `expiry` case and the word \"expiry\" from the final message instead of keeping an assertion that proves nothing extra.",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 3,
      "protected": false,
      "trigger": "Running `tests/agent-git-tamper.mjs` after a change that breaks `expire`'s `git_tamper` catch (lib/agent.js:608) or the `expired.gitTamper` return in `waitWorker`: the suite still passes because the `expiry` iteration takes the same path as `list`.",
